From fa1a9d4fee215bde036d27520af7a5dc13c9f311 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 06:11:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10269/11362] Enforce scope declarations, ordered by wound-wait
 (REQ-INFRA-6820)

A declaration used to bind only its author. It constrained what THAT session
could stage and did nothing about a stranger staging the same path -- advisory in
exactly the direction that hurt. The shared index took one agent's staged work
into another agent's commit three times on 2026-08-29, once taking the worktree
import guard while its commit message was being written.

Enforcement was not simply switched on, because it had been tried and removed.
Cross-session judging DEADLOCKED: A declares a.py, B declares b.py, B stages its
own b.py, and A's record refuses it. That is why judging became per-session.

Wound-wait supplies the missing ordering. The OLDEST declaration owns a contested
path; ties break on run id. Oldest-wins is deadlock-free by construction because
the order is total and time-based, so no cycle can form. Tie-breaking on run id
makes it deterministic, so the wounded session is told exactly what the winner
believes -- a rule where each side thinks it won is worse than no rule. The
wounded session keeps its edits and re-declares: we wound the CLAIM, never the
work, because Fission can treat an abort as an early commit only where writes are
prefix-safe and a half-applied source edit is not.

TWO PROPERTIES STOP THIS FREEZING THE REPOSITORY, and both are tested.

A pattern naming no literal path segment is advisory to strangers. The conductor
holds a standing declaration of `*` because it sweeps all uncommitted work; that
states what it will COMMIT, not that nobody else may touch anything. Enforced
literally, no agent could ever stage a file again. Verified against the live
declaration on disk: a stranger staging arbitrary paths is not contested.

Claims expire after four hours. Agents die without releasing their scope
routinely, and a killed run leaves its declaration behind forever, so without
expiry the first crash would lock its paths permanently. Fail toward NOT
blocking: a wrongly-held claim stops real work with nobody to appeal to, while a
wrongly-released one costs attribution on a single commit.

An unidentified committer is exempt rather than held to every claim. That is
deliberately the opposite of the seal rule beside it: applied there, a missing
environment variable becomes a repository-wide freeze.

Declaration time also warns when an older claim overlaps what was just asked for,
so a session learns before doing the work rather than after. The first version of
that probe substituted "x" for wildcards and found NOTHING -- `scripts/*` became
`scripts/x`, which the literal `scripts/target.py` it genuinely collides with
does not match. It now probes with the concrete paths each side named, in both
directions.

Verified end to end in a scratch repo: younger session warned at declaration,
refused when staging the older session's file, permitted when staging its own,
and the owner permitted throughout. 14 unit tests; 7 mutations RED (enforcement
removed, youngest-wins, universal patterns enforceable, staleness never expiring,
undated claims sorting oldest, one-direction overlap probing, older sessions
warned), restore byte-identical, 46 existing harness tests still green.

